{"paper":"D. Eppstein, Faster Evaluation of Subtraction Games, FUN 2018 (arXiv:1804.06515)","checks":[{"id":"nim-prefix","where":"§1","claim":"The nim-value sequence starts 0,1,0,1,2,…,3,2,3,4,0 (35 terms)","ok":true,"detail":"35 / 35 terms match the mex table"},{"id":"cold-prefix","where":"§1","claim":"The cold positions start 0,2,5,7,10,12,…,85,95 (21 terms)","ok":true,"detail":"21 / 21 terms match"},{"id":"sdf-maximal","where":"§1","claim":"The cold set is a maximal square-difference-free set (sieve = nim-value 0)","ok":true,"detail":"sieve vs nim-value 0: 0 mismatches below 2^20; no square difference among the 2053 cold positions below 2^16 (all 2106378 pairs); every hot n < 2^20 has a square move to a cold one (0 exceptions)"},{"id":"oeis-a030193","where":"OEIS","claim":"Cold positions = A030193 b-file (61300 terms, up to 8388562)","ok":true,"detail":"61300 / 61300 terms match"},{"id":"oeis-a014586","where":"OEIS","claim":"Nim-values = A014586 b-file (10001 terms)","ok":true,"detail":"10001 / 10001 terms match"},{"id":"lemma2","where":"Lemma 2","claim":"Nim-value t+1 positions = cold positions of the game with hotspots {nim ≤ t}","ok":true,"detail":"layered sieve vs mex DP below 2^16: 0 mismatches (max nim-value 63)"},{"id":"theorem2","where":"Theorem 2","claim":"Nim-values = repeated convolution-based cold sets with hotspots (m+1 rounds)","ok":true,"detail":"31 rounds below 2^13, 0 mismatches"},{"id":"theorem1","where":"Theorem 1","claim":"Divide and conquer with Boolean convolution finds the cold positions","ok":true,"detail":"convolution vs sieve below 2^20: 0 mismatches"},{"id":"m-le-S","where":"§1","claim":"m ≤ |S|: a nim-value never exceeds the number of available moves","ok":true,"detail":"0 positions below 2^20 exceed ⌊√n⌋"},{"id":"maxnim-exp","where":"§3.1, Fig. 1","claim":"Max nim-value up to n ≈ c·n^0.351 (Siegel repeated median, n < 2^24)","ok":true,"detail":"450 record points, fitted exponent 0.3507, c = 1.321; max 450 at n = 16669564"},{"id":"cold-exp","where":"§3.2, Fig. 2","claim":"|C ∩ [0,n)| grows like n^0.7, at least n^0.69 (cubes n ≤ 2^30)","ok":true,"detail":"1024 cube points, fitted exponent 0.6983 (c = 0.898); local slope between 2^29 and 2^30: 0.6949; 1816355 cold positions below 2^30"},{"id":"golomb-lb","where":"§1 (Golomb Thm 4.1)","claim":"A maximal square-difference-free subset of [0,n] has Ω(√n) elements","ok":true,"detail":"n ≤ |C ∩ [0,n)|·(⌊√(n−1)⌋+1) at every cube; min |C|/√n = 1.000 (and it keeps growing)"},{"id":"ruzsa-205","where":"§1","claim":"(1 + log_205 12) / 2 ≈ 0.733412","ok":true,"detail":"= 0.7334118"},{"id":"base5-exp","where":"§3.3","claim":"A perfect base-5 strategy gives a set of size n^{log_10 25} ≈ n^0.71534","ok":false,"detail":"as printed log_10 25 = 1.39794 (> 1, impossible for a subset of [0,n]); the number 0.71534 is log_25 10 = 0.71534 (10 of 25 digit pairs kept). Exponent and base are swapped"},{"id":"residues","where":"§3.3","claim":"Squares mod 13 are 0,±1,±3,±4; ±3 = 4²,6²,7²,9²; {0,2} mod 5 and {0,2,7} mod 13 are square-difference-free; mod 7 every pair differs by a square","ok":true,"detail":"squares mod 13 = {0,1,3,4,9,10,12}, roots of ±3 = {4,6,7,9}, pairs mod 7 with no square difference either way: 0"},{"id":"base5-ones","where":"§3.3, Fig. 3","claim":"Base 5: the ones digit lies in {0,2} with a few exceptions","ok":true,"detail":"97.72 % of 1816355 cold positions; 41422 exceptions, 41378 of them digit 4 (counts 839372 / 8 / 935561 / 36 / 41378); exception share 2^14: 13.18 %, 2^18: 10.23 %, 2^22: 7.78 %, 2^26: 5.17 %, 2^30: 2.28 %"},{"id":"base5-fives","where":"§3.3, Fig. 3","claim":"Base 5: the fives digit shows no significant non-uniformity","ok":true,"detail":"max relative deviation from 1/5: 1.45 % (counts 358295 / 362905 / 368556 / 363870 / 362729)"},{"id":"base5-25s","where":"§3.3, Fig. 3","claim":"Base 5: the twenty-fives digit is non-uniform and possibly heading to {0,2}","ok":true,"detail":"share of {0,2} in the 25s digit: 2^14: 54.9 %, 2^16: 54.4 %, 2^18: 56.8 %, 2^20: 58.6 %, 2^22: 59.9 %, 2^24: 60.4 %, 2^26: 60.5 %, 2^28: 60.4 %, 2^30: 60.2 % (uniform = 40 %); it stops rising after 2^26"},{"id":"base7","where":"§3.3, Fig. 4","claim":"Base 7: no significant non-uniformity in the low digit","ok":true,"detail":"max relative deviation from 1/7: 0.22 % (counts 259466 / 260031 / 259302 / 259294 / 259417 / 258917 / 259928)"},{"id":"base13","where":"§3.3, Fig. 4","claim":"Base 13: the low digit is converging towards {0,2,7}","ok":true,"detail":"three most frequent digits {0,2,7}; share of {0,2,7}: 2^14: 47.2 %, 2^18: 52.4 %, 2^22: 64.6 %, 2^26: 71.8 %, 2^30: 75.2 % (uniform = 23.1 %)"},{"id":"golomb-20000","where":"§3.3","claim":"Golomb: among the first 20,000 positions the low base-5 digits of cold positions are highly non-uniform","ok":true,"detail":"911 cold positions below 20,000; 85.7 % end in base-5 digit 0 or 2 (uniform = 40 %)"},{"id":"mdb-formula","where":"§3.3 (Golomb)","claim":"Moser–de Bruijn subtraction game: nim-value = base-4 digits mod 2, read in binary","ok":true,"detail":"0 mismatches below 2^20"},{"id":"mdb-sqrt","where":"§3.3","claim":"Moser–de Bruijn game: cold count and max nim-value are Θ(√n)","ok":true,"detail":"below 2^20: 1024 cold positions (= √n), max nim-value 1023, |S| = 1024"},{"id":"breakeven-nim","where":"§3.1","claim":"n^1.35 log₂²n < n^1.5 needs n ≈ 10^26","ok":true,"detail":"crossover at n ≈ 10^25.77"},{"id":"breakeven-cold","where":"§3.2","claim":"n log₂²n < n^1.2 needs n ≈ 10^18","ok":true,"detail":"crossover at n ≈ 10^17.70"}],"timings":[{"logN":12,"dp":8.383958000000348,"sieve":0.4243750000005093,"conv":6.111750000000029},{"logN":14,"dp":20.5853330000009,"sieve":1.368625000000975,"conv":39.54999999999927},{"logN":16,"dp":55.00795900000048,"sieve":3.1320830000004207,"conv":325.34649999999965},{"logN":18,"dp":545.1308749999989,"sieve":8.318957999999839,"conv":1508.8558329999996},{"logN":20,"dp":4649.39625,"sieve":42.57600000000093,"conv":7512.206833}],"recordsFit":{"e":0.350735